<div class="prose series-introduction"><p>In the same Northstar incident, the agent uses accepted definition {{CASE_DEFINITION_VERSION}} and its bounded permission to investigate the report. Comparing the required identifiers with output {{CASE_OUTPUT}} confirms {{CASE_MISSING_COUNT}} omissions.</p><p><strong>Provenance</strong> makes the basis of that result inspectable: the source versions, operations, responsible recorder and standing at each step. The definition and concrete example below connect that trail to the meaning and permission introduced in the authority article.</p></div>
<section class="article-section" id="incident-case" aria-labelledby="incident-case-heading"><h2 id="incident-case-heading">The incident under examination</h2>{{INCIDENT_CASE}}</section>
<section class="article-section" id="provenance-definition" aria-labelledby="provenance-definition-heading"><h2 id="provenance-definition-heading">What provenance means</h2>
{{PROVENANCE_DEFINITION}}
</section>
{{VISUAL_PROVENANCE}}
<section class="article-section" id="evidence" aria-labelledby="evidence-title"><h2 id="evidence-title">What the trail lets you conclude</h2>
<div class="prose"><p>The comparison establishes a failure against the accepted coverage criterion for {{CASE_ACCOUNT}}, snapshot {{CASE_SNAPSHOT}} and its declared filters. The agent preserves that comparison and its limitations in an <strong>Evidence Set</strong>, including counterevidence and gaps. A claim about the cause of the omission needs further evidence.</p><p>A source becomes evidence through a declared relationship to a claim, identifying the subject version, scope and conditions for continued use. The <strong>Failure Assessment</strong> keeps occurrence, cause, impact, containment and recovery separately reported. The owner can inspect both what the comparison establishes and what still needs investigation.</p></div>
<h3 class="article-subheading">Follow the evidence into the assessment</h3>
{{FIGURE_EVIDENCE}}
<h3 class="article-subheading">Keep the evidence records distinct</h3>
<div class="prose"><p>A <strong>Measurement Method</strong> defines how to count and compare the rows. A <strong>Measurement Result</strong> records one execution. A <strong>Finding</strong> states a conclusion within the support of an Evidence Set or Research Run. An <strong>Operational Observation</strong> records what was seen during ordinary project work; the research-specific Observation record belongs to research or experiment runs.</p><p>A <strong>digest</strong> is a digital fingerprint of a file’s contents. It helps identify the exact file used as evidence. It does not establish that the file’s contents or a conclusion are correct.</p></div>
<div class="case-note"><strong>A bounded conclusion</strong>“This export omitted rows that the comparison rule required. We have not yet established the cause.”</div>
{{SOURCES_PROVENANCE}}
</section>
<section class="article-section" id="provenance-agent" aria-labelledby="provenance-agent-title"><h2 id="provenance-agent-title">How an agent uses the trail</h2>
<ol class="series-process"><li><strong>Recover the exact subject</strong><p>Identify the export run, software revision, input snapshot and relevant definition.</p></li><li><strong>Inspect the comparison</strong><p>Check the method, saved output and required identifiers, including uncertainty and contrary evidence.</p></li><li><strong>State the supported result</strong><p>Keep conclusions within those conditions and identify the evidence still needed for a causal explanation.</p></li></ol>
<h3 class="article-subheading">What the trail preserves</h3>
<div class="prose"><p>A provenance trail can identify who recorded or transformed information. Whether that participant may authorize a change depends on the authority records for that scope. Likewise, a copied definition can have a faithful source trail while still requiring its own acceptance.</p></div>
<p class="series-bridge">The owner now has a bounded assessment with a recoverable trail. The next article examines the existing acceptance history and the separate routes for a proposed change, view refresh and incident closure.</p>
</section>
